## 11. Server 85 Walkthrough — My Boardfarm Config

Style: **one server block, path-based routing**, plain HTTP on the IP.

`/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/bodfarm`:

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name 10.63.68.173;          # matches requests whose Host is the IP

    # React frontend — everything not matched below
    root /home_ldap/ahazra/workspace/boardfarm_frontend/dist;
    index index.html;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;    # SPA fallback
    }

    # FastAPI — all API routes
    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;    # no trailing slash → FastAPI receives /api/...
        proxy_set_ Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }

    # Swagger docs and OpenAPI schema
    location /docs {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
    }

    location /redoc {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
    }

    location /openapi.json {                 # Swagger UI at /docs fetches this
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
    }
}
```

```
http://10.63.68.173/               → React (static files from dist/)
http://10.63.68.173/devices/5      → React (try_files → index.html)
http://10.63.68.173/api/...        → FastAPI on 127.0.0.1:8000
http://10.63.68.173/docs           → FastAPI Swagger UI
http://10.63.68.173/redoc          → FastAPI ReDoc
http://10.63.68.173/openapi.json   → FastAPI schema
```

Notes:
- `server_name` can be an **IP**. If someone uses a hostname instead (e.bun00085`), the Host won't match → nginx falls back to the `default_server` (Debian ships `sites-enabled/default` with `listen 80 default_server;` showing the "Welcome to nginx" page). Check `ls -l /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/`.
- `www-data` needs traverse permission on `/home_ldap/ahazra/...` (see 2.13).
- `127.0.0.1:8000` must be where the backend is actually reachable **on the host**: uvicorn running directly on the host, or a Docker container published to host port 8000 (`-p 127.0.0.1:8000:8080`). An earer session mentioned port 9090 — whichever it is, `proxy_pass` must point to the **host-side** port. Verify with `ss -tlnp | grep -E '8000|9090'`.

### 11.1 Server 14 vs Server 85 — two deployment styles

| | Server 14 | Server 85 |
|---|---|---|
| Routing by | **domain name** (`server_name`) | **URL path** (`location`) |
| Frontend URL | `https://cpetools-dev.upclabs.com/` | `http://10.63.68.173/` |
| API URL | `https://cpetools-api-dev.upclabs.com/...` | `http://10.63.68.173/api/...` |
| `server {}` bs per app | separate for frontend and API | one for both |
| Same-origin? | **No** → CORS headers needed | **Yes** → no CORS needed |
| DNS entries needed | one per app | none (uses IP) |
| HTTPS | yes (wildcard cert) | no (plain HTTP) |

Both are valid and use exactly the same nginx mechanisms. The choice is organizational, not a technical necessity.

---

## 12. The Browser Flow — Frontend Download, Then API Calls

### 12.1 Step 1 — browser downloads `index.html`

User types `http://10.63.68.173/`rowser → nginx:   GET / HTTP/1.1
                   Host: 10.63.68.173

nginx: server_name 10.63.68.173 matches
       location / → try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html
       "/" is not a file → falls to /index.html
       reads .../boardfarm_frontend/dist/index.html from disk, sends it
```

`index.html` is a small skeleton:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <script src="/assets/main-abc123.js"></script>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/style-def456.css">
</head>
<body>
  <div id="root"></diody>
</html>
```

### 12.2 Step 2 — browser downloads JS and CSS

The browser sees the tags and makes **more requests**:

```
GET /assets/main-abc123.js     → location / → try_files finds dist/assets/main-abc123.js → sent
GET /assets/style-def456.css   → same → sent
```

Now the **entire React app is loaded and running in the browser**. Nginx's frontend job is done — it only served files. (The hash in the filename, `abc123`, changes every build so browsers never use a stale cached copy.)

### the React app calls the API (separate requests)

```javascript
// inside a React component
useEffect(() => {
  fetch('/api/devices/')
    .then(res => res.json())
    .then(data => setDevices(data));
}, []);
```

`fetch('/api/devices/')` triggers a **new, separate HTTP request**. A relative URL is completed by the browser using the current page's origin → `http://10.63.68.173/api/devices/`:

```
Browser → nginx:   GET /api/devices/ HTTP/1.1
                   Host: 10.63.68.173

nginx: location /api/ â_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000
       FastAPI handles /api/devices/, returns JSON
       nginx relays JSON back → React renders the device list
```

**So yes:** the browser downloads the whole frontend first (HTML + JS + CSS), then the JavaScript running in the browser makes separate fetch/XHR requests. Every request carries its path (and Host), and nginx uses them to decide: static file or backend?

```
Time ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────►
 GET /                     → index.html        (nginx, from disk)
 GET /assets/main.js       → JS bundle         (nginx, from disk)
 GET /assets/style.css     → CSS               (nginx, from disk)
        [React starts running in the browser]
 GET /api/devices/         → JSON              (nginx → FastAPI)
 POST /api/devices/5/reset → JSON              (nginx → FastAPI)
 ... more API calls as the user clicks ...
```

### 12.4  separate domains → cross-origin → CORS

There the frontend is on `cpetools-dev.upclabs.com` and the code calls a **full URL** on another domain:

```javascript
fetch('https://cpetools-api-dev.upclabs.com/api/devices/')
```

Browser does a DNS lookup → same IP `10.63.69.36` → same nginx → different `Host` → API server block.

**Origin = scheme + host + port.** `https://cpetools-dev.upclabs.com` ≠ `https://cpetools-api-dev.upclabs.com` → **cross-origin**. Browsers enforce **CORS**: JS may only read the response if the server returns `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` permitting it.

For "non-simple" requests (e.g. with an `Authorization` header or `Content-Type: application/json`), the browser first sends a **preflight**:

```http
OPTIONS /api/devices/ HTTP/1.1
Host: cpetools-api-dev.upclabs.com
Origin: https://cpetools-dev.upclabs.com
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
Access-Control-Request-Headers: authorization, content-type
```

The nginx `if ($request_method = OPTIONS)` block answers it directly:

```http
HTTP/1.1 200 OK            (or 204 on mv3tools)
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, DELETE, OPTIONS, HEAD
Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization, Origin, X-Requested-With, Content-Type, Accept
```

Only then does the browser send the real POST. CORS is enforced **by the browser** only — `curl` ignores it.

**Server 85:** page and API are both `http://10.63.68.173` → **same origin** → no CORS needed. That's a big advantage of path-based routing.

- 13. Docker, `0.0.0.0` vs `localhost`, Port Publishing

### 13.1 The chain when the backend runs in Docker

```
Internet / internal network
        │
        ▼
   ┌─────────────────────────────────┐
   │  Host machine (Ubuntu)          │
   │                                 │
   │  nginx (port 80)                │  ← systemd-managed, pre-installed
   │    │  proxy_pass to             │
   │    │  127.0.0.1:<hosport>     │
   │    ▼                            │
   │  Docker published port          │  ← e.g. -p 9090:8080 in docker run
   │    │  forwards to               │
   │    ▼                            │
   │  ┌─────────────────────────┐    │
   │  │ Container (172.17.0.3)  │    │
   │  │ FastAPI on 0.0.0.0:8080 │    │
   │  └─────────────────────────┘   ────────────────────────────┘
```

1. Request arrives at host port 80 → nginx.
2. nginx matches `location /api/` → `proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:9090` (the **published host port**).
3. Docker forwards host `9090` → container IP `172.17.0.3` port `8080`.
4. uvicorn inside the container, bound to `0.0.0.0:8080`, accepts it.
5. Response travels back: container → Docker → nginx → browser.

`-p HOST:CONTAINER` → `-p 9090:8080` means "host por container port 8080". **nginx's `proxy_pass` uses the HOST port**, never the container port.

### 13.2 Why the container app MUST bind `0.0.0.0`

In bridge mode a container has its **own network namespace** with (at least) two interfaces:

- `lo` — `127.0.0.1`, reachable only from inside the container
- `eth0` — e.g. `172.17.0.3`, how Docker's port forwarding reaches it

Docker forwards traffic to the **container's IP (`eth0`)**, not its loopback.

- App binds `127.0.0.1:8080` → listens only on the cer's loopback → forwarded traffic arrives on `eth0` → nobody listening → **connection refused**. App looks "up" but is unreachable.
- App binds `0.0.0.0:8080` → listens on `lo` **and** `eth0` → forwarded traffic accepted ✅

```dockerfile
CMD ["uvicorn", "app.main:app", "--host", "0.0.0.0", "--port", "8080"]
```

`localhost` only makes sense for something that must **never** be reached from outside its own machine/namespace (e.g. a helper daemon).

### 13.3 Security: `0.0.0.0` inside the containeand safe

What's actually reachable depends on:
- (a) the Docker **port mapping** (only published ports are exposed),
- (b) the **host firewall**,
- (c) **nginx** in front.

⚠️ `-p 9090:8080` publishes on the host's `0.0.0.0:9090` — reachable from the network **directly, bypassing nginx** (and Docker's iptables rules can bypass `ufw`). To force all traffic through nginx, bind the published port to the host loopback:

```bash
docker run -p 127.0.0.1:9090:8080 my-fastapi-image
```

```yaml
# docker-compl
ports:
  - "127.0.0.1:9090:8080"
```

Now only nginx (on the same host) can reach it.

### 13.4 Without Docker (uvicorn directly on the host)

```bash
uvicorn app.main:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000
```

Here `127.0.0.1` is actually the *better* choice: nginx is on the same machine, so it can reach loopback, and nobody else can bypass nginx. (In a container the rule flips, because "same machine" for the container means "inside the container".)

### 13.5 Who manages what

| Thing | Managed by | My responsibility? |
|---|---|---|
| nginx install + unit file | platform/provisioning, systemd | no |
| nginx site config (`sites-enabled/boardfarm`) | me (with sudo) | yes |
| Docker container / uvicorn | me | yes |
| Port alignment (`proxy_pass` ↔ published port) | me | yes |
| `--host 0.0.0.0` in container | me | yes |

---

## 14. Advanced — `internal`, `auth_request`, `location = /__whoami`

So far every request came **from a client**. But nginx can also make requests **to itself** — internal redirects arequests. That's what `internal`, `auth_request`, `@named` locations and `/__whoami` are about.

### 14.1 External vs internal requests

| | External request | Internal request |
|---|---|---|
| Who starts it | the browser / curl | nginx itself |
| Visible to client? | yes | no — happens inside nginx |
| Examples | `GET /api/devices/` | `try_files` fallback to `/index.html`, `error_page`, `auth_request` subrequest, `X-Accel-Redirect` |

I've already used one without knowing: `try_files $uri $uri/ /index.hl;` — the jump to `/index.html` is an **internal redirect**. The browser asked for `/devices/5` and never learns nginx actually served `/index.html`.

### 14.2 `internal` — a location clients can't reach

```nginx
location /protected_files/ {
    internal;                 # only internal redirects/subrequests may use this location
    alias /srv/files/;
}
```

- Browser requests `GET /protected_files/report.pdf` directly → **404**.
- nginx itself (via `auth_request`, `error_page`, `X-Accel-Redirect`, iles`, `rewrite`) → allowed.

Think of it as a staff-only door inside the building: customers can't open it, staff can.

### 14.3 Named locations `@name` — always internal

```nginx
location @login {
    return 302 /login?next=$request_uri;
}
```

A named location can't be addressed by any URL at all; it's only a target for `try_files`, `error_page`, etc. Example:

```nginx
location / {
    try_files $uri @backend;      # file not found → hand off to the backend
}
location @backend {
    proxy_pass ht27.0.0.1:8000;
}
```

### 14.4 `error_page` — internal redirect on an error code

```nginx
error_page 404 /404.html;          # show a custom page (internal redirect, client still sees 404)
error_page 401 = @login;           # "=" → response code comes from @login (here: 302 redirect)
error_page 502 503 504 /maintenance.html;
```

### 14.5 `auth_request` — nginx asks "is this user allowed?" before every request

**The problem:** I want `/api/` (or the whole site) protected by login, but I don't want eackend/static location to implement auth itself.

**The idea:** before handling a request, nginx makes an **internal subrequest** to an auth endpoint. The auth endpoint's **status code** decides:

| Subrequest returns | nginx does |
|---|---|
| **2xx** (200, 204) | ✅ allow — continue with the original request |
| **401** or **403** | ❌ deny — returns that code to the client (can be caught with `error_page`) |
| anything else | 💥 500 Internal Server Error |

The auth endpoint's **body is ignored**atus (and optionally headers) matter.

> Who really checks credentials, the exact phase-by-phase flow, cookies/tokens, shared secrets, roles, trust boundaries and when this design fits → **Section 15**.

### 14.6 `location = /__whoami` — the auth endpoint

```nginx
location = /__whoami {
    internal;                                     # browsers can't call it directly
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000/auth/whoami; # FastAPI checks the session/token
    proxy_pass_request_body off;                  n't send the original body (e.g. big uploads)
    proxy_set_header Content-Length "";
    proxy_set_header X-Original-URI    $request_uri;     # tell auth which URL was requested
    proxy_set_header X-Original-Method $request_method;
}
```

Why it's written this way:
- **`=`** → exact match, highest priority; nothing else can shadow it, and it's the fastest match.
- **`/__whoami`** with double underscore → a naming convention so it can't collide with a real app or React route (no one builds a page call/__whoami`).
- **`internal`** → clients can't probe it; only `auth_request` uses it.
- The original request's headers (including **Cookie** and **Authorization**) are passed to the subrequest by default — that's how the backend sees the session.
- `proxy_pass` here **has a URI part** (`/auth/whoami`), so the backend receives `/auth/whoami`, not `/__whoami` (trailing-URI rule, 9.6).

### 14.7 Full working example (server 85 style)

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name 10.63.68.173;

    root e_ldap/ahazra/workspace/boardfarm_frontend/dist;
    index index.html;

    # ── Public: the login page and auth API must NOT require login ──
    location /api/auth/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
    }

    # ── Protected API ──
    location /api/ {
        auth_request /__whoami;                          # 1. ask first
        auth_request_set $auth_user $upstream_http_x_user;   # 2. capture X-User header from the auth response
        proxy_set_header X-User $auth_user;              # 3. pass identity to the real backend

        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }

    # ── The internal auth check ──
    location = /__whoami {
        internal;
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/auth/whoami;
        proxy_pass_request_body off;
        proxy_set_header Content-Lengt      proxy_set_header X-Original-URI $request_uri;
    }

    # ── Frontend (left public here so the login page can load) ──
    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
    }

    # ── Unauthenticated → JSON 401 for the API ──
    error_page 401 = @unauthorized;
    location @unauthorized {
        default_type application/json;
        return 401 '{"detail":"Not authenticated"}';
    }
}
```

For a classic server-rendered site you'd instead redirect to a login page:

```ngx
error_page 401 = @login;
location @login {
    return 302 /login?next=$request_uri;
}
```

### 14.8 The matching FastAPI endpoint

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Request, Response, HTTPException

app = FastAPI()

def verify_session(token: str | None):
    # look up the session / decode the JWT; return a user object or None
    ...

@app.get("/api/auth/whoami")
def whoami(request: Request):
    user = verify_session(request.cookies.get("session"))
    if user is None:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=401)          # nginx → deny
    # 2xx → nginx allows; the header is captured by auth_request_set
    return Response(status_code=204, headers={"X-User": user.username})

@app.get("/api/devices/")
def devices(request: Request):
    user = request.headers.get("X-User")              # identity injected by nginx
    return {"user": user, "devices": [...]}
```

### 14.9 Flow of one protected request

```
Browser:  GET /api/devices/   Cookie: session=abc
   │
   ▼
nginx: location /api/ matched uth_request /__whoami
   │
   ├──► INTERNAL subrequest: GET /__whoami  (Cookie: session=abc copied along)
   │        location = /__whoami → proxy_pass → FastAPI /api/auth/whoami
   │        FastAPI: session valid → 204, X-User: ahazra
   │◄──── 204
   │
   │  2xx → allowed. auth_request_set: $auth_user = "ahazra"
   │
   ├──► proxy_pass → FastAPI /api/devices/   (X-User: ahazra)
   │◄──── 200 JSON
   ▼
Browser receives JSON.  (It never saw /__whoami.)

If the session is invalid:
   /__whoami → 401 → nginx stops → error_page 401 → @unauthorized → client gets 401 JSON
   /api/devices/ backend is never even called.
```

Every protected request costs one extra subrequest. For heavy traffic you can cache auth results (`proxy_cache` keyed on the cookie) — usually unnecessary on an internal tool.

Check that the module is present (both packages normally include it):

```bash
nginx -V 2>&1 | grep -o with-http_auth_request_module
```

### 14. common `/__whoami` pattern — a public "who am I" for the frontend

Sometimes `/__whoami` is **not** internal — it's a small endpoint the React app calls on startup to learn the logged-in user:

```nginx
location = /__whoami {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/auth/whoami;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
}
```

```javascript
const me = await fetch('/__whoami').then(r => r.ok ? r.json() : null);
if (!me) window.location = '/login';
```

How to tell which pattern a config uses: **`internal;` prese → it's an auth_request target; **absent** → the browser is meant to call it.

### 14.11 `X-Accel-Redirect` — backend decides, nginx delivers the file

Another use of `internal`: protected downloads. The backend checks permissions, then tells nginx "serve this file" without streaming it through Python.

```nginx
location /api/ {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
}

location /protected_files/ {
    internal;
    alias /srv/boardfarm/logs/;
}
```

```python
@app.get("/api/logs/{name}")
def get_log(ntr, request: Request):
    if not user_can_read(request, name):
        raise HTTPException(403)
    return Response(headers={"X-Accel-Redirect": f"/protected_files/{name}"})
```

nginx sees the `X-Accel-Redirect` header in the backend's response → internally redirects to `/protected_files/<name>` → serves the file from disk efficiently. Clients can't hit `/protected_files/` directly because it's `internal`.

### 14.12 Summary of internal machinery

| Feature | Starts an internal request when... | Typicse |
|---|---|---|
| `try_files ... /index.html` | file not found | SPA routing |
| `error_page CODE target` | a response has that code | custom error pages, login redirect |
| `auth_request /x` | before every request in that location | central authentication |
| `X-Accel-Redirect` header | backend sends that header | protected downloads |
| `internal;` | — (marks a location as internal-only) | hide auth endpoints/files |
| `@name` | — (always internal) | fallback handlers |

---

## 15. Authentication  nginx — The Full Picture

Section 14 showed the *mechanics* of `auth_request`. This section answers the questions that come right after: who really authenticates, how a request flows through it exactly, what happens to cookies and tokens, whether backends need shared secrets/DBs, and when this design is right or wrong.

### 15.1 Authentication vs authorization

| | Question | Where it can live |
|---|---|---|
| **Authentication** | "Who are you? Is your session/token/login valid?" | gateway (nginx + authervice) **or** app |
| **Authorization** | "May *this* user do *this* action on *this* object?" (e.g. delete test session 42) | **the app** — it's business logic |

When an architecture diagram puts "Authentication" on the API Gateway / Load Balancer box (next to SSL termination, routing, rate limiting, load balancing), it means the first row: reject requests without valid credentials **before** they reach any backend. It's the standard API-gateway feature list (nginx, F5, Kong, AWS API Gateway, Envoy alldvertise it). It does not mean the gateway decides app permissions.

### 15.2 Who actually checks the credentials?

**With `auth_request`, nginx does NOT authenticate. It only enforces the verdict.** The auth service (Flask, FastAPI, anything — e.g. on port 9000) checks the cookie or token against LDAP or the DB and answers **2xx, 401, or 403**. Nginx just obeys the answer.

The exceptions — where **nginx itself** checks credentials:

| Method | Who checks | Typical use |
|---|---|---|
| `auth_request`  external auth service; nginx obeys its status | SSO, LDAP, sessions, JWT via a service |
| `auth_basic` | **nginx**, against a password file | quick protection for internal tools |
| mTLS (`ssl_verify_client`) | **nginx**, during TLS: client must present a cert signed by your CA | machine-to-machine, high security |
| `auth_jwt` | **nginx**, validates JWT signature/expiry — **NGINX Plus only** (commercial) | API gateways on NGINX Plus |
| `allow` / `deny` | **nginx**, by client IP — access control, notntity | "only the office network" |

**`auth_basic`** — browser shows a username/password popup:

```bash
sudo apt install apache2-utils               # provides htpasswd
sudo htpasswd -c /etc/nginx/.htpasswd ahazra # -c creates the file; omit -c to add more users
```

```nginx
location /admin/ {
    auth_basic           "Restricted";
    auth_basic_user_file /etc/nginx/.htpasswd;
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
    proxy_set_header Host   $host;
    proxy_set_header X-User $remote_user;     # nginxnows the username after basic auth
}
```

(Only safe over HTTPS — basic auth sends the password base64-encoded, i.e. readable, on every request.)

**mTLS** — the client proves identity with a certificate:

```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    ssl_certificate        /etc/nginx/ssl/server.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key    /etc/nginx/ssl/server.key;
    ssl_client_certificate /etc/nginx/ssl/company-ca.crt;   # CA that signed allowed client certs
    ssl_verify_client      on;                            no valid client cert → 400, never reaches a location

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
        proxy_set_header X-Client-DN $ssl_client_s_dn;      # e.g. "CN=ci-runner-01,O=Company"
    }
}
```

**`auth_jwt`** (NGINX Plus only — open-source nginx does JWT checks via `auth_request` to a small service):

```nginx
location /api/ {
    auth_jwt          "api";
    auth_jwt_key_file /etc/nginx/jwks.json;   # public keys to verify signatures
    proxy_set_header  X-User $jwt_claim_su# claims become variables
    proxy_pass        http://127.0.0.1:8000;
}
```

**IP rules combined with a password — `satisfy any`:**

```nginx
location /internal/ {
    satisfy any;                  # pass if EITHER check passes (default is "all")
    allow 10.63.0.0/16;           # office network: no password needed
    deny  all;
    auth_basic "Restricted";      # everyone else: password
    auth_basic_user_file /etc/nginx/.htpasswd;
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
}
```

### 15.3 Exactly how a ruest flows through `auth_request` (corrected)

My first mental model, checked line by line:

| What I thought | Reality |
|---|---|
| nginx checks the Host matches, then the path matches (exact or partial), then goes inside that block | ✅ Correct — first `listen` IP:port, then `server_name`, then the best `location` (9.2) |
| "if the **first line** is `auth_request`..." | ❌ **Line order inside a location does not matter.** nginx config is declarative; nginx processes every request in fixed **phases**._request` belongs to the *access* phase, `proxy_pass` to the *content* phase, so auth always runs first wherever it's written. It can even be written once at `server {}` level and inherited. |
| the auth URL is defined in a location in **another server block**, and since it's `internal` the host doesn't matter | ❌ **The subrequest is resolved inside the SAME server block.** `internal` means "clients can't call it", not "global". Every server block that uses `auth_request /__auth` needs its own `location =__auth` — write it once in a snippet and `include` it (example below). What *can* live anywhere is the auth **service** that location proxies to. |
| the auth location proxies to the auth server, the response comes back, nginx captures it into variables we define | ✅ Correct — `auth_request_set $var $upstream_http_<header>;` The **status code** decides allow/deny. |
| then it returns to the same place and "hits the next line", the `proxy_pass` | ⚠️ Conceptually yes — control returns to the origi which moves on to its **next phase** (content), where `proxy_pass` runs. Not "next line". |
| it forwards the entire request with the same payload and modified headers | ✅ Correct — body untouched (it was never sent to the auth service), original headers plus whatever `proxy_set_header` adds or overrides |

**The phases (simplified), in the order nginx runs them for every request:**

```
1. pick server block        (listen, server_name)
2. rewrite phase (server)   rewrite / return / set / if   at servevel
3. find location            (9.2 rules)
4. rewrite phase (location) rewrite / return / set / if   inside the location
5. ACCESS phase             allow/deny → auth_basic → auth_request (+auth_jwt)   (combined per "satisfy")
6. try_files
7. CONTENT phase            proxy_pass / uwsgi_pass / static file
8. log phase                access_log
   (+ response filters: add_header etc. applied to the response on its way out)
```

**⚠️ Pitfall that follows from the phases — `return` runs BEFORE auth:*x
location /admin/ {
    auth_request /__auth;
    return 200 "secret stuff";   # served to EVERYONE: return is rewrite phase (4), auth is access phase (5)
}
```

The same fact is *useful* for CORS: `if ($request_method = OPTIONS) { return 204; }` answers preflights before auth — correct, since preflights never carry credentials.

**The refined flow, step by step:**

```
Request:  GET /sessions/42   Host: gw.example.com   Cookie: session=abc

 ① Server selection     listen 443 + server_name gw.example.c    → server block "gw"
 ② Location selection   longest prefix /sessions/                     → location /sessions/
 ③ Rewrite phase        no return/rewrite here                        → continue
 ④ Access phase         auth_request /__auth
      ├─ nginx creates an INTERNAL SUBREQUEST to /__auth
      │    resolved in the SAME server block → location = /__auth { internal; ... }
      │    it carries the original headers (Cookie, Authorization) but no body
      ├─ that location ps to the auth service (http://10.7.2.50:9000/verify)
      ├─ auth service checks session=abc against LDAP/DB
      │    → 204   X-User: ahazra   X-Roles: qa,admin
      ├─ nginx reads the STATUS: 2xx → allowed   (401/403 → stop & deny; other → 500)
      └─ auth_request_set copies X-User / X-Roles into $user / $roles
 ⑤ Content phase        proxy_pass http://compute2:8001
      └─ new request to the backend: same method, path, body, client headers
         + proxy_set_header X-U-Roles $roles
 ⑥ Response             backend 200 JSON → nginx → browser
                        (the browser never sees /__auth or the auth service)
```

The complete config for that flow, shared across several server blocks:

```nginx
# /etc/nginx/snippets/auth.conf   — written once
location = /__auth {
    internal;
    proxy_pass http://10.7.2.50:9000/verify;        # auth service, can be on another host
    proxy_pass_request_body off;
    proxy_set_header Content-Length "";
    proxy_set_headeinal-URI    $request_uri;   # the auth service otherwise only sees "/verify"
    proxy_set_header X-Original-Method $request_method;
}
```

```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name gw.example.com;
    # ssl_certificate ... ;

    include snippets/auth.conf;                     # pasted here → location = /__auth exists in THIS block

    location /sessions/ {
        auth_request /__auth;
        auth_request_set $user  $upstream_http_x_user;
        auth_request_set $roles $upstream_http_x_ros;
        proxy_set_header Host    $host;
        proxy_set_header X-User  $user;
        proxy_set_header X-Roles $roles;
        proxy_pass http://compute2:8001;
    }

    location /tools/ {
        auth_request /__auth;
        auth_request_set $user  $upstream_http_x_user;
        auth_request_set $roles $upstream_http_x_roles;
        proxy_set_header Host    $host;
        proxy_set_header X-User  $user;
        proxy_set_header X-Roles $roles;
        proxy_pass http://compute2:8002;
    }
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name ai.example.com;
    include snippets/auth.conf;                     # needed AGAIN — different server block
    location / {
        auth_request /__auth;
        auth_request_set $user $upstream_http_x_user;
        proxy_set_header Host   $host;
        proxy_set_header X-User $user;
        proxy_pass http://ai-backend:8000;
    }
}
```

**It runs on every request.** nginx keeps no memory between requests: every request to a protected location triggers its own au subrequest (unless you add caching, 15.7).

### 15.4 Which header goes where — the three directives

This is the root of most cookie confusion. Three directives, three directions:

| Directive | Direction | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `proxy_set_header X v` | nginx → **backend** (request) | add/override a header on the request nginx sends upstream |
| `add_header X v` | nginx → **browser** (response) | add a header to the response the browser receives |
| `auth_request_set $v $upstream_http_x` | auth s → **nginx variable** | capture a header from the auth subrequest's response |

```
                  Browser
        ▲ response                    │ request
        │ (+ add_header ...)          │ Cookie, Authorization, Host
        │                             ▼
   ┌───────────────────────── nginx ──────────────────────────┐
   │  auth_request /__auth ───────────────► auth service :9000 │
   │       ◄─── status + X-User, Set-Cookie                    │
   │       (dropped unless captured with auth_request_set)     │
   │                                                           │
   │  proxy_set_header X-User $user ──────► backend :8001      │
   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────â`

So: **`add_header Set-Cookie ...` DOES tell the browser** (it's on the response). **`proxy_set_header`** is what tells the backend.

### 15.5 Can the auth service set cookies or issue tokens through `auth_request`?

**Mostly no — by default.** For the auth subrequest:
- its **body is thrown away**;
- its **headers are NOT sent to the browser** — including `Set-Cookie`;
- only its **status** (and whatever you capture with `auth_request_set`) is used.

So if `/verify` returns a refreshed session cookthe browser never gets it — unless you forward it by hand:

```nginx
location /api/ {
    auth_request /__auth;
    auth_request_set $new_cookie $upstream_http_set_cookie;   # capture from the AUTH response
    add_header Set-Cookie $new_cookie;                         # put it on the response to the BROWSER
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
}
```

This works and is how "silent session refresh" is sometimes done (oauth2-proxy uses this idea), but it's fiddly:
- Capturing **multiple** `Set-Cookie` heads through one variable is unreliable — keep it to one cookie.
- `add_header` is only added on success-type responses (200, 201, 204, 206, 301, 302, 303, 304, 307, 308) unless you append `always`.
- `add_header` inheritance: a location with its own `add_header` loses all inherited ones (e.g. security headers set at server level) — same rule as `proxy_set_header` (9.10).
- If the backend response also sends `Set-Cookie`, the browser receives both — make sure they don't fight.

**The normal design:** logfresh and logout are **ordinary, non-internal endpoints** that the browser calls directly through nginx. They are *not* behind `auth_request` (you can't require login to log in). The internal verify endpoint only verifies.

```nginx
# Public — the browser calls these directly; their Set-Cookie reaches the browser normally
location /auth/ {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:9000;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
}

# Internal — only answers yes/no
location = /__auth {
    internal;
    proxy_pass http://120.1:9000/auth/verify;
    proxy_pass_request_body off;
    proxy_set_header Content-Length "";
}

# Protected
location /api/ {
    auth_request /__auth;
    auth_request_set $user $upstream_http_x_user;
    proxy_set_header X-User $user;
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8001;
}
```

```
1. POST /auth/login {user, password}  → auth service checks LDAP → 200 + Set-Cookie: session=abc
   (normal proxied request → the browser stores the cookie)
2. GET /api/devices   Cookie: session=abc
   → auth_request h → verify(session=abc) → 204 X-User: ahazra → proxied to 8001
3. Session nearly expired → frontend calls POST /auth/refresh → new Set-Cookie (normal request again)
4. POST /auth/logout → auth service deletes the session → Set-Cookie with expiry in the past
```

### 15.6 Cookies and the browser — the "server address" worry

**Doubt:** "If the auth service sets a cookie, won't the browser distrust it because it came from a different server/port?"

No. **The browser only ever talks to nginx.** It has no idea a service on port 9000 exists. From the browser's point of view, the `Set-Cookie` came from the host in the URL bar (e.g. `10.63.68.173`), so it stores it for that host and sends it back on every later request to that host. The hops behind nginx are invisible.

Cookie rules worth knowing:
- Cookies are scoped by **domain + path, NOT by port** (unlike origins, which include the port).
- No `Domain` attribute → "host-only": sent only to the exact host that set it.
- `Domain=.upclabs.com` →t to **every** `*.upclabs.com` — the way to share one login across `cpetools-dev`, `mv3tools-dev`, ...
- `HttpOnly` → JavaScript can't read it (protects against XSS stealing the session). Use it for session cookies.
- `Secure` → only sent over HTTPS. **Pitfall on server 85:** it's plain `http://`, so browsers refuse to store `Secure` cookies there.
- `SameSite=Lax` (browser default) → not sent on most cross-site requests.
- **Cross-origin fetch with cookies** (server 14 style, frontend and API on didomains) needs `fetch(url, {credentials: 'include'})` **and** the API to answer `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` with a **specific** origin — `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` is **not allowed** together with credentials. Server 14's `*` configs therefore only work for token-in-header auth, not cookies.

Also: the browser's `Cookie` header is forwarded by nginx to **both** the auth subrequest and the real backend (default behaviour). The backend sees the cookie too — it just doesn't need to underd it.

### 15.7 Do backends need to decrypt tokens? Shared secret? Shared DB? Lots of network calls?

**Doubt:** "The backend doesn't know how to decrypt the access token, so every service needs the JWT secret, a shared user DB, refresh tokens... isn't that getting out of hand?"

In the gateway pattern: **no.** That's the point of it. Backends never touch tokens:

```
Auth service (9000):  LDAP, user DB, JWT secret, refresh tokens, sessions   ← ALL of it, in ONE place
Backends (8001/8002): see only "X-Use ahazra", "X-Roles: qa,admin"        ← trust it, nothing else
```

The three models, compared:

| Model | Backend does | Needs secret? | Needs user DB? | Network call per request |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Gateway trust** (`auth_request` + identity headers) | reads `X-User` | no | no | 1 subrequest nginx → auth service |
| **Asymmetric JWT** (OIDC: Keycloak, Azure AD, Okta) | verifies signature itself with the **public** key (JWKS URL) | no — public key only | no | none (keys cached) |
| **Opaque tokntrospection** | asks the auth server "is token X valid?" | no | no | 1 call per request — exactly what `auth_request` does once on behalf of all backends |

A **shared symmetric JWT secret** across services is the version that gets out of hand — and the industry answer is asymmetric signing (private key signs, public key verifies), not sharing secrets or databases. **Refresh tokens only ever live with the auth service / identity provider**; services never see them.

**Cost of the extra call:** it's oneal HTTP call, usually a millisecond or two. If it matters, cache verdicts in nginx:

```nginx
# in http {}
proxy_cache_path /var/cache/nginx/auth levels=1 keys_zone=authcache:10m max_size=50m inactive=10m;

location = /__auth {
    internal;
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:9000/auth/verify;
    proxy_pass_request_body off;
    proxy_set_header Content-Length "";

    proxy_cache           authcache;
    proxy_cache_key       "$http_authorization$cookie_session";  # one cache entry per credential
    proxy_cache_valid     200 204 30s;                           # remember "allowed" for 30s
    proxy_ignore_headers  Cache-Control Expires;                 # cache even if the auth service says no-cache
}
```

Trade-off: after logout, a cached "allowed" can survive up to 30 s. (Responses carrying `Set-Cookie` aren't cached by default.)

### 15.8 Roles — identity roles vs app permissions

| Kind | Example | Lives in |
|---|---|---|
| **Identity-level roles** — company-wide facts | LDAP groups: "in QA team", "is n" | auth service → passed as `X-Roles` |
| **App-specific permissions** | "may delete *this* test session", "owns *this* board" | **each app's own DB**, keyed by the username/ID from `X-User` |

So service 8001 does **not** need the auth DB. It keeps a small permissions table that references users by name. **Central identity, local permissions** — that split is the whole design.

```python
# FastAPI backend behind the gateway
from fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, Header, HTTPException

app = FastAPI()
 current_user(x_user: str = Header(...), x_roles: str = Header("")):
    return {"name": x_user, "roles": set(filter(None, x_roles.split(",")))}

@app.delete("/sessions/{sid}")
def delete_session(sid: int, user=Depends(current_user)):
    session = db_get_session(sid)                                   # app's own DB
    if session.owner != user["name"] and "admin" not in user["roles"]:
        raise HTTPException(403)                                    # authorization = app logic
    db_delete_session(sid)
    return {"deleted": sid}
```

### 15.9 The trust boundary — the part that makes or breaks gateway auth

Backends trust `X-User` blindly. That is only safe if **nothing except nginx can reach them.**

**⚠️ Pitfall 1 — bypassing nginx:** if `10.7.2.48:8001` is reachable from the network, anyone can call it directly with `X-User: admin`. Ways to close it (pick at least one):

| Option | How |
|---|---|
| Same machine, loopback | backend binds `127.0.0.1` (or Docker `-p 127.0.0.1:8001:8000`) — simprivate network + firewall | backends on another host, firewall allows only the gateway's IP |
| Docker internal network | backends have no published ports; nginx container on the same network |
| Shared secret header | nginx adds `proxy_set_header X-Gateway-Token "<long random>";`, backend rejects requests without it |
| mTLS gateway → backend | `proxy_ssl_certificate` on nginx; backend requires that client cert |

So backends do **not** have to be on the same machine — they have to be **unreachable excvia the gateway**. Loopback is just the easiest way to guarantee that.

**⚠️ Pitfall 2 — client-supplied identity headers:** a client can send `X-User: admin` *to nginx*.
- In a protected location, `proxy_set_header X-User $user;` **replaces** whatever the client sent — safe.
- In a location **without** `auth_request` that proxies to the same backend, the client's header passes straight through! Blank it explicitly:

```nginx
location /public/ {
    proxy_set_header X-User  "";      # empty value = ot sent upstream
    proxy_set_header X-Roles "";
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8001;
}
```

Backend-side belt and braces:

```python
import os
from fastapi import Header, HTTPException

GATEWAY_TOKEN = os.environ["GATEWAY_TOKEN"]

def from_gateway(x_gateway_token: str = Header(None)):
    if x_gateway_token != GATEWAY_TOKEN:
        raise HTTPException(401, "Requests must come through the gateway")
```

**⚠️ Pitfall 3 — `return` in a protected location skips auth** (15.3).

### 15.10 When gateway a right — and when it's not

| Situation | Where authentication belongs |
|---|---|
| One UI + one backend that are one product | **In the app.** Gateway does only TLS + routing. |
| A product shipped to many customers / infrastructures | **In the app**, with an optional "trust proxy header" mode |
| One company, many internal tools for the same employees (the architecture-diagram case) | **Gateway / central identity provider** for "is this a logged-in employee?"; each app keeps its own permissions |
| Extnal systems calling your API | **Standard tokens or API keys** validated by your app — never gateway headers |

**One product (UI + backend):** keep auth in the app. The recommended pattern for a same-origin web app is **BFF (Backend-for-Frontend)**: the browser holds only an `HttpOnly` session cookie; the server keeps the session (and any LDAP/OIDC tokens) on its side. Access + refresh tokens in the browser add little here; they earn their place with mobile apps, third-party API clients, cross-domain sets and stateless microservices. The UI being a separately **built** SPA (like server 85's React `dist/`) is fine — what matters is same origin + same session, so UI and backend behave as one application.

**Product for many customers:** don't make it depend on one company's gateway. Mature products ship full built-in auth **plus** an optional proxy-auth mode. Grafana is the textbook example:

```ini
# grafana.ini — only enabled when a customer has an SSO gateway in front
[auth.proxy]
enabled = true
heademe = X-WEBAUTH-USER
header_property = username
auto_sign_up = true
whitelist = 127.0.0.1        ; only trust the header from the gateway's address
```

**Defense in depth is common:** the gateway does the coarse check (TLS, rate limiting, "must be an authenticated employee") *and* each app still validates and authorizes. Not wasteful — cautious.

### 15.11 Integrating with external services

**Rule:** gateway-injected headers (`X-User`, `X-Roles`) are an **internal contract inside one trust boundary** (oncompany, one deployment, one gateway). They must never become your external API contract — an external team should not have to "accept certain headers" to work with you.

An external service usually expects **its own** credentials (API key, OAuth token, username/password, its own session). `auth_request` cannot log your users into someone else's system. Three options:

**(a) nginx injects a static service credential** — possible, limited:

```nginx
location /ext/jira/ {
    auth_request /__auth;                               # our user must be logged in with US
    proxy_pass https://jira.example.com/;                   # trailing / → strips /ext/jira/ (9.6)
    proxy_set_header Host jira.example.com;                 # the EXTERNAL name — NOT $host (9.10)
    proxy_ssl_server_name on;                               # send SNI to the external HTTPS server
    proxy_set_header Authorization "Bearer <service-token>";
    proxy_set_header Cookie "";                             # never leak our users' cookio a third party
    proxy_set_header X-User "";
}
```

Pitfalls: the external service sees **every** request as one service account (no per-user identity); the secret sits in `/etc/nginx` (restrict file permissions); no error handling, retries, or response transformation; every external API change becomes an nginx change.

**(b) Your backend's integration layer — usually the right choice:**

```
Browser → nginx → my backend /api/integrations/... → (server-side call with token) → external service
`hon
import os
import httpx
from fastapi import APIRouter, Depends, HTTPException

router = APIRouter(prefix="/api/integrations")
JIRA_URL = os.environ["JIRA_URL"]
JIRA_TOKEN = os.environ["JIRA_TOKEN"]          # secret stays in the backend, never in the browser

@router.get("/jira/issues/{key}")
async def jira_issue(key: str, user=Depends(current_user)):   # my app's own auth
    if not can_use_jira(user):                                # my app's own permissions
        raise HTTPException(403)
    async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=10) as client:
        r = await client.get(f"{JIRA_URL}/rest/api/2/issue/{key}",
                             headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {JIRA_TOKEN}"})
    if r.status_code == 404:
        raise HTTPException(404, "Issue not found")
    r.raise_for_status()
    data = r.json()
    return {"key": data["key"], "summary": data["fields"]["summary"]}   # reshape for my UI
```

Why it's better: per-user permission checks, secrets in one place, error handling and retries, response shaping, logging/audit — and the external service only needs its normal, standard auth. Nothing about it depends on nginx.

**(c) Per-user delegation** — when the external system must know *which* user acts: OAuth2 (the user grants your app access; you store their token server-side) or token exchange via a shared identity provider.

**Other teams calling *your* API:** issue them an API key or OAuth2 client-credentials token, sent as a standard `Authorization: Bearer ...` header, validated by your.

### 15.12 Pitfalls checklist

| ✔ | Check |
|---|---|
| ☐ | Backends unreachable except through nginx (loopback / firewall / internal network / gateway secret / mTLS) |
| ☐ | Identity headers blanked (`proxy_set_header X-User "";`) in every non-protected location that proxies to those backends |
| ☐ | No `return` inside protected locations (it runs before auth) |
| ☐ | `location = /__auth` exists (via `include`) in **every** server block that uses `auth_request` |
| ☐ | `/auth/login`, `/auth/auth/logout` are public, normal locations — not behind `auth_request` |
| ☐ | Auth endpoint returns only 2xx / 401 / 403 (anything else = 500 for the user) |
| ☐ | `Set-Cookie` from the auth subrequest is forwarded only if intended (`auth_request_set` + `add_header ... always`) |
| ☐ | Locations re-declare all needed `proxy_set_header` / `add_header` (inheritance is all-or-nothing) |
| ☐ | Session cookies `HttpOnly`; `Secure` only works over HTTPS; `Domain=` set if subdomains must share the login oss-origin + cookies → specific origin + `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` (never `*`) |
| ☐ | Authorization (who may do what) stays in each app |
| ☐ | External integrations use standard credentials; gateway headers stay internal |

---

## 16. Command Cheat Sheet

### Processes & service

```bash
systemctl status nginx              # is it running, since when, master + workers (CGroup)
systemctl cat nginx                 # unit file in effect (incl. overrides)
cat /usr/lib/systemd/system/nginice
systemctl is-enabled nginx          # starts at boot?
sudo systemctl reload nginx         # graceful config reload (master stays)
sudo systemctl restart nginx        # full stop + start
sudo journalctl -u nginx -n 50      # service logs
pgrep -a nginx                      # real nginx processes only
ps -ef | grep "[n]ginx:"            # same, without matching the grep itself
ps -ef --forest | less              # process tree (see PPIDs)
cat /run/nginx.pid                  # master PID
nproc                               # CPU cores → number of workers with "auto"
```

### Config

```bash
sudo nginx -t                       # test config syntax
sudo nginx -T                       # test + dump the FULL expanded config (proof of include = paste)
sudo nginx -T | grep -n server_name # list all virtual hosts
sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx   # safe reload
ls -l /etc/nginx/conf.d/ /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
grep -E "worker_processes|^user" /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
```

### Package / build

```bash
ngx -v ; nginx -V 2>&1 | tr ' ' '\n'
apt-cache policy nginx
dpkg -S /usr/lib/systemd/system/nginx.service
```

### Network

```bash
ip addr                             # my interfaces and IPs
ss -tlnp                            # who listens on which address:port
sudo ss -tlnp | grep nginx
getent hosts mv3tools-dev.upclabs.com
dig +short mv3tools-dev.upclabs.com
curl -v http://10.63.68.173/api/
curl -v --resolve mv3tools-dev.upclabs.com:443:10.63.69.36 https://mv3tools-dev.upclabs.com/
curl -vk -H "Host: mv3tools-dev.upclabs.com" https://10.63.69.36/
curl -v http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/  # hit the backend directly, skipping nginx
```

### Logs

```bash
sudo tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log
sudo tail -f /var/log/nginx/error.log      # FIRST place to look for 502/403/404 causes
```

### Docker

```bash
docker ps                                  # see PORTS column, e.g. 127.0.0.1:9090->8080/tcp
docker port <container>
docker logs -f <container>
```

### Permissions

```bash
namei -l /home_ldap/ahazra/workspace/boardfarm_frontend/dist/index.html
sudo -u www-data cat /home_ldap/ahazra/workspace/boardfarm_frontend/dist/index.html
```

