## 17. Troubleshooting — Error → Cause

| Symptom | Likely cause | Check |
|---|---|---|
| **502 Bad Gateway** | backend not running / wrong port in `proxy_pass` / app bound to `127.0.0.1` inside container | `error.log` ("connect() failed ... Connection refused"), `ss -tlnp`, `curl 127.0.0.1:PORT` |
| **504 Gateway Timeout** | backend too slow | raise `proxy_read_timeout` / `uwsgid_timeout` |
| **403 Forbidden** on static files | `www-data` can't traverse a parent dir / read the file | `namei -l <path>`, `error.log` "Permission denied" |
| **404 on refresh** of a React route | missing `try_files ... /index.html` | config |
| **404 from FastAPI** for `/api/...` | `proxy_pass` trailing-slash mismatch vs FastAPI route prefix | 9.6, `curl 127.0.0.1:8000/api/...` |
| **Wrong site / "Welcome to nginx"** | Host didn't match any `server_name` → default server used | `nginx -T | grep -n seer_name`, `sites-enabled/default` |
| **413 Request Entity Too Large** | upload larger than `client_max_body_size` | raise it in that server block |
| **CORS error in browser console** | cross-origin call without `Access-Control-Allow-*` headers | 12.4; or switch to same-origin path routing |
| **Config change has no effect** | not reloaded, or reload failed due to syntax error | `sudo nginx -t`, then reload |
| **Backend reachable from network, bypassing nginx** | Docker published on `0.0.0.0` | publish as `127.0.0.1:HOST:CONTAINER` |
| **500 on every protected request** | auth_request endpoint returned something other than 2xx/401/403 (e.g. 404 because the path is wrong) | `error.log`, `curl` the auth endpoint directly |
| **400 Bad Request / "Invalid HTTP_HOST header"** (Django) | Host sent to backend not in `ALLOWED_HOSTS` (often missing `proxy_set_header Host $host;`) | 9.10 |
| **Redirect sends browser to `127.0.0.1:8000`** | backend saw `Host: 127.0.0.1:8000` | add `proxy_set_header Host $host;` (9.10) |
| **CSRF "Origin checking failed"** | Host / `X-Forwarded-Proto` don't match what the browser used | 9.10 |
| **Protected location served without login** | `return` inside it (runs before auth), or identity header passed through from client | 15.3, 15.9 |
| **Cookie from auth service never reaches browser** | subrequest headers are dropped | `auth_request_set` + `add_header ... always`, or make login a normal endpoint (15.5) |
| **Session cookie not stored on server 85** | `Secure` cookie over plain HTTP | 15.6 |
| **Security headers disappear in one location** | `add_header`/`proxy_set_header` inheritance is all-or-nothing | 9.10, 15.5 |

---

## 18. FAQ — Every Question I Asked, Answered

**Q: Why do my two servers run nginx two different ways? I didn't change anything.**
Different **packages**: server 85 has Ubuntu/Debian's nginx package, server 14 has nginx.org's. Each package ships its own unit file, default config, and service user. Chosen when the machine was provisioned. Same program, same behavir. (Sections 3–4)

**Q: Server 14 shows `-c /etc/nginx/nginx.conf`, server 85 doesn't. Different config files?**
No. Both load `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf`. Server 14's unit passes it explicitly with `-c ${CONFFILE}`; server 85 relies on the compiled-in default (`--conf-path` in `nginx -V`). (4.4)

**Q: What's `-g 'daemon on; master_process on;'`?**
Global directives given on the command line instead of in the file: run in background, use master/worker model. (4.2)

**Q: Did I install or start nginx?**
No. It'an OS service installed by the platform/provisioning, managed by systemd, auto-started at boot (`enabled`). Root master + persistent workers since the boot date is the signature of a systemd daemon. (3.4, 4.5)

**Q: Why did `ps -ef | grep nginx` on server 14 show uwsgi, celery, python?**
They run as the Linux **user** named `nginx`; grep matched the username column. They're separate Django apps, not the web server. (2.5, 10.5)

**Q: Who creates the worker processes, and why 8 here and 4 there?**
The master forks them. `worker_processes auto;` = one per CPU core: server 85 has 8 cores, server 14 has 4. (5.1–5.3)

**Q: Why does the master run as root and workers as `www-data`/`nginx`?**
Root is only needed to bind ports 80/443. Workers handle untrusted traffic, so they run as a low-privilege user for security. (5.6)

**Q: How does reload work without downtime?**
Master validates new config, starts new workers, old workers finish and exit; master never stops. (5.7)

**Q: Why do the `nginx.conf` files differ (`w-data` vs `nginx`, `sites-enabled`, `modules-enabled`)?**
Packaging again — Debian vs nginx.org defaults. The site-specific parts live in included files. (6.4)

**Q: Is `include` like C's `#include` — copies everything into one config before starting?**
Yes. Files are pasted in place (glob expanded alphabetically), then nginx parses one big config. `sudo nginx -T` shows the result. (7)

**Q: One nginx, many `server {}` blocks with different names — different IPs or ports?**
Neither. Same IP, same pory're distinguished by the **`Host` header** (domain name) matched against `server_name`. That's virtual hosting. (8)

**Q: All the `*.upclabs.com` names resolve to the same IP — so what's different?**
Only the name. DNS maps every name to `10.63.69.36`; the browser puts the name in `Host` (and SNI for TLS); nginx routes by it. (8.5–8.7)

**Q: Why can several files all have `location /`?**
`location` is scoped to its own `server {}` block. Nginx enters exactly one block per request, then matches locationly inside it. (9.1)

**Q: How does nginx pick a location?**
Exact `=` → longest prefix (`^~` stops) → regex in order → longest prefix. (9.2)

**Q: Does the browser download everything and then make separate calls with the full URL?**
Yes. It downloads `index.html`, then the JS/CSS it references; the React app then runs in the browser and makes separate `fetch` requests. A relative URL like `/api/devices/` is completed with the current origin; on server 14 the code uses a full URL to the API domain. Ngutes each request by Host + path. (12)

**Q: Why does server 14 have all those `Access-Control-Allow-*` headers but server 85 doesn't?**
Server 14's frontend and API are on different domains → cross-origin → browser requires CORS headers (and sends OPTIONS preflights). Server 85 serves both from one origin → no CORS. (12.4)

**Q: Why `--host 0.0.0.0` in the container and not `localhost`?**
Docker forwards traffic to the container's `eth0` IP, not its loopback. `127.0.0.1` inside the container = unreac `0.0.0.0` = listen on all interfaces including `eth0`. Safe, because exposure is controlled by port mapping, firewall, nginx. (13.2–13.3)

**Q: Which port goes in `proxy_pass` when using Docker?**
The **host-side** published port (left side of `-p HOST:CONTAINER`). (13.1)

**Q: What are `internal`, `auth_request`, `location = /__whoami`?**
`internal` = location only nginx itself may use. `auth_request` = before handling a request, nginx subrequests an auth URL; 2xx allows, 401/403 denies. `location = /__oami` = the conventional exact-match, internal auth endpoint that proxies to the backend's "who is this user" check. (14)

**Q: Who sets the `Host` header — is it a fixed header from nginx?**
The **client** sets it, from the URL that was typed/requested. Nginx only reads it (`$host`, `$http_host`). It can be faked with `curl -H`, so it's for routing, not security. (8.9)

**Q: Can a request move from one server block to another?**
No. Each request lands in exactly one block (by `listen`, then `server_name` and stays there. Share behaviour via the same upstream or an `include`d snippet. (8.10)

**Q: Why `proxy_set_header Host $host;` — what happens without it?**
Proxying is a second, new HTTP request. By default nginx sets its Host to the `proxy_pass` address (`127.0.0.1:8000`), so the backend generates wrong redirects/links, and Django may reject it (ALLOWED_HOSTS, CSRF). The line copies the user's real Host. (9.10)

**Q: Is "Authentication" on an nginx/API-gateway box valid, or nonsense?**
Valid and commo— it means "reject requests without valid credentials before they reach any backend". Authorization (who may do what) stays in the app. (15.1)

**Q: Does nginx itself authenticate with `auth_request`?**
No — it only enforces the verdict (2xx allow, 401/403 deny) from an auth service. nginx checks credentials itself only with `auth_basic`, mTLS, and `auth_jwt` (NGINX Plus). (15.2)

**Q: Does the `/__auth` location have to be in the same server block?**
Yes — the subrequest is resolved in the same blockernal` doesn't make it global. Put it in a snippet and `include` it in each block. The auth *service* can be anywhere. (15.3)

**Q: Does the order of lines (auth_request first, proxy_pass after) matter?**
No — nginx runs fixed phases; access (auth) always precedes content (proxy_pass). But `return` runs *before* auth, so it bypasses it. (15.3)

**Q: Can the auth service set a cookie / issue a token via `auth_request`?**
Not by default: the subrequest's body is discarded and its headers don't reach the broer. You can forward one header manually with `auth_request_set` + `add_header`. Normally login/refresh are ordinary public endpoints. (15.5)

**Q: `add_header Set-Cookie` — does that tell the backend or the browser?**
The **browser** (response). `proxy_set_header` is what goes to the backend. (15.4)

**Q: Will the browser distrust a cookie set by the auth service on another port/server?**
No — the browser only sees nginx; cookies are scoped by domain + path, not port. (15.6)

**Q: Do all backends need tWT secret, a shared user DB, refresh tokens?**
Not in the gateway pattern — only the auth service holds them; backends read `X-User`. If services verify tokens themselves, use asymmetric JWT (public keys), not shared secrets. (15.7)

**Q: Must all services be on the same machine on localhost?**
They must be **unreachable except via nginx**; loopback is simply the easiest way. Firewalls, internal networks, a gateway secret header, or mTLS also work. (15.9)

**Q: For one UI + backend product, should auth bet the gateway?**
No — keep it in the app (BFF: HttpOnly session cookie). Gateway auth fits many services sharing one user base. Products for many customers should have built-in auth plus an optional proxy-header mode (like Grafana). (15.10)

**Q: How do I integrate with external services that need their own credentials?**
Preferably through my backend's integration layer (`/api/integrations/...`) which calls them server-side with a token; nginx-injected static credentials are possible but limited. Gatewayeaders never become an external contract. (15.11)

